---
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const manifest = `import { defineDynamicManifest } from '@wesflo/vite-plugin-lockxy';

export default defineDynamicManifest({
  id: 'foo-api',
  endpoints: [
    {
      id: 'get-foo',
      label: 'Get foo',
      method: 'GET',
      path: '/api/foo/:id',
      handler: ({ params, searchParams }) => ({
        status: 200,
        body: {
          id: params.id,
          detailed: searchParams.get('details') === 'true',
        },
      }),
    },
    {
      id: 'create-foo',
      label: 'Create foo',
      method: 'POST',
      path: '/api/foo',
      handler: async ({ request }) => ({
        status: 201,
        body: {
          id: crypto.randomUUID(),
          ...(await request.json()),
        },
      }),
    },
  ],
});`;
---

<DocsLayout
    title="Dynamic responses"
    description="Generate request-aware local API responses with JavaScript or TypeScript handlers."
    activePath="/dynamic-responses/"
>
    <article>
        <p class="kicker">Advanced response handling</p>
        <h1>Generate responses from request data.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            JavaScript and TypeScript manifests add one handler per endpoint for cases where static files are not enough.
            Use route parameters, query parameters, headers, or a request body to build the response locally.
        </p>

        <Callout title="Keep simple mocks simple">
            <p>
                Naming conventions and declarative JSON or YAML manifests remain the preferred workflow. Use dynamic
                handlers only when the response genuinely depends on request data.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <h2 id="manifest-module">Manifest module</h2>
        <p>
            Create <code>mock/mock.manifest.ts</code> or <code>mock/mock.manifest.js</code> and default-export a dynamic
            manifest. Every endpoint requires a <code>path</code> and a <code>handler</code>. IDs, labels, methods, and the
            active flag use the same rules as declarative endpoints. Dynamic endpoints do not contain scenarios.
        </p>
        <Code code={manifest} label="mock/mock.manifest.ts" />

        <h2 id="handler-context">Handler context</h2>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>request</code></td><td>A standard Web Request. Read JSON, text, or form data with its normal methods.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>params</code></td><td>Decoded values from required and optional <code>:parameters</code> in the matched path.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>searchParams</code></td><td>The request URL's <code>URLSearchParams</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>method</code></td><td>The normalized HTTP method.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>pathname</code></td><td>The request pathname without its query string.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>A request body is a stream and can be consumed only once. Call only one of <code>json()</code>, <code>text()</code>, or <code>formData()</code> for a request.</p>

        <h2 id="handler-response">Handler response</h2>
        <p>A handler may be synchronous or asynchronous and returns an object with these optional fields:</p>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td><code>200</code></td><td>An HTTP status from 100 through 599.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>delay</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td>Fixed milliseconds or an inclusive random range.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>headers</code></td><td>Inferred</td><td>Additional response headers. A supplied content type wins.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>body</code></td><td>Empty</td><td>An object, array, string, Buffer, or empty value.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            Objects and arrays are serialized as JSON, strings as plain text, and Buffers as binary data. Lockxy still
            owns the final response, so logging, delays, the <code>x-lockxy</code> header, and bodyless
            <code>HEAD</code>, <code>204</code>, and <code>304</code> responses remain consistent.
        </p>

        <h2 id="resolution-and-reload">Resolution and reload</h2>
        <p>The default manifest priority is:</p>
        <Code code={`mock.manifest.json
mock.manifest.yaml
mock.manifest.yml
mock.manifest.ts
mock.manifest.js`} label="highest to lowest priority" />
        <p>
            Set <code>manifestFileName: 'mock.manifest.ts'</code> to select the module explicitly. Vite compiles TypeScript;
            Lockxy caches the loaded manifest at runtime and reloads it when the watched module changes.
        </p>
        <Callout title="File synchronization" tone="warning">
            <p>Delayed Docker or remote file synchronization can briefly expose the previous mock state.</p>
        </Callout>

        <h2 id="panel-integration">Panel integration</h2>
        <p>
            The manifest route exposes only serializable endpoint metadata, never handler source code. The panel displays
            <strong>Dynamic response</strong> instead of a scenario selector. Endpoint toggles and bypass cookies continue
            to use the endpoint ID.
        </p>

        <h2 id="best-practices">Best practices</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Use stable endpoint and manifest IDs when the proxy panel stores selections.</li>
            <li>Keep handlers small and deterministic unless randomness is part of the behavior being tested.</li>
            <li>Return synthetic data only; dynamic manifests execute locally as trusted development code.</li>
            <li>Prefer static files for fixed responses so backend colleagues can inspect them without reading code.</li>
            <li>Treat thrown handler errors as implementation failures. Lockxy logs them and responds with a controlled 500.</li>
        </ul>
    </article>
</DocsLayout>
